From c7b0ae6389f398788bd91831e551784d51dbd8d3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 23:52:04 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] opcodes: microblaze: annotate mbar sleep modes

With the previous patch, objdump prints mbar 16, 8 and 24 as the
sleep, hibernate and suspend pseudo-instructions, and every other mbar
as mbar with its immediate.  Some of those others also enter a sleep
mode.  On the mbar page of UG984, IMM & 24 selects the mode, and IMM & 1
and IMM & 2 skip the waits for instruction-side and data-side accesses.
So mbar 17 is a sleep that does not wait for instruction-side
accesses, and a reader who sees "mbar 17" has to work that out from
the manual.

This adds a comment after the immediate in that case, in the "// " form
the disassembler already uses for branch targets:

	mbar	17		// sleep, without the instruction-side wait
	mbar	26		// suspend, without the data-side wait
	mbar	31		// suspend, without either wait

mbar 16, 8 and 24 still print as sleep, hibernate and suspend.  An mbar
that enters no sleep mode, where IMM & 24 is zero, prints as before.
The pseudocode does not use IMM & 4, so mbar 20 gets a plain "// sleep";
I have not checked what hardware does with that bit.

The test the last patch of this series adds has mbar 9, 17, 18, 20, 26
and 31, with their comments.

Tested on microblaze-elf. gas stays at 332 expected passes and 10
unsupported, binutils at 245 expected passes, 2 expected failures, 18
untested and 17 unsupported, and ld at 480 expected passes, 15 expected
failures, 28 untested and 236 unsupported.  No unexpected failures.
With the whole series applied, GDB, which disassembles through
print_insn_microblaze, shows the same comments.

opcodes/
	* microblaze-dis.c (print_insn_microblaze) <INST_TYPE_IMM5>: Name
	the sleep mode and the skipped waits of an mbar that enters one.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 opcodes/microblaze-dis.c | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/opcodes/microblaze-dis.c b/opcodes/microblaze-dis.c
index c4fa6e2e493..6e0957c029f 100644
--- a/opcodes/microblaze-dis.c
+++ b/opcodes/microblaze-dis.c
@@ -439,6 +439,28 @@ print_insn_microblaze (bfd_vma memaddr, struct disassemble_info * info)
 	  /* For mbar insn.  */
 	case INST_TYPE_IMM5:
 	  print_func (stream, "\t%s", get_field_imm5_mbar (&buf, inst));
+	  {
+	    /* When IMM & 24 is nonzero, mbar enters a sleep mode (UG984,
+	       mbar): 16 is sleep, 8 hibernate and 24 suspend.  mbar 16, 8
+	       and 24 themselves match the sleep, hibernate and suspend
+	       pseudo-instructions, which come earlier in microblaze_opcodes,
+	       so an IMM with those bits set that gets here also has some of
+	       its low bits set.  Name the mode, and say which waits the low
+	       bits skip: IMM & 1 skips the wait for instruction-side
+	       accesses, and IMM & 2 the wait for data-side accesses.  */
+	    static const char *const mode[] = {
+	      NULL, "hibernate", "sleep", "suspend"
+	    };
+	    static const char *const skips[] = {
+	      "", ", without the instruction-side wait",
+	      ", without the data-side wait", ", without either wait"
+	    };
+	    unsigned int imm = (inst & IMM5_MBAR_MASK) >> IMM_MBAR;
+
+	    if ((imm & 0x18) != 0)
+	      print_func (stream, "\t\t// %s%s", mode[(imm >> 3) & 3],
+			  skips[imm & 3]);
+	  }
 	  break;
 	  /* For mbar 16 or sleep insn.  */
 	case INST_TYPE_NONE:
